video or symbols. Offer questions and

211
00:19:22.196 --> 00:19:26.852
meeting information beforehand, simple
choices where useful, and enough

212
00:19:26.853 --> 00:19:31.509
processing time. Notice the sensory
environment. Build on the student's

213
00:19:31.510 --> 00:19:36.765
strengths and interests. Behaviour may
communicate preferences or difficulty, so

214
00:19:36.766 --> 00:19:41.622
observe carefully and check your
interpretation wherever possible. Trusted

215
00:19:41.623 --> 00:19:46.812
supporters can assist communication while
keeping the student's own perspective

216
00:19:46.813 --> 00:19:51.935
central. What a student tells us needs to
influence what we do. With families,

217
00:19:51.936 --> 00:19:56.991
begin with the student's strengths,
interests and what works. Listen to their

218
00:19:56.992 --> 00:20:01.648
knowledge without assuming you already
know their experience. Use plain

219
00:20:01.649 --> 00:20:06.173
language, explain school terms and respect
differences in culture and

220
00:20:06.174 --> 00:20:10.630
perspective. Some parents may be
neurodivergent themselves, so check

221
00:20:10.631 --> 00:20:15.554
communication preferences rather than
making assumptions. Use real examples

222
00:20:15.555 --> 00:20:20.544
from school to discuss needs, goals,
participation and possible adjustments.

223
00:20:20.545 --> 00:20:25.667
Check shared understanding, agree next
steps and keep the conversation open as

224
00:20:25.668 --> 00:20:30.457
circumstances change. This extends the
consultation work we introduced in

225
00:20:30.458 --> 00:20:35.580
Session Four. Choose one practice from
each column that you could try with the

226
00:20:35.581 --> 00:20:39.838
student and family you have in mind. Pause
to note those choices.

227
00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:52.301
As you look at your two choices, check
what they would make easier for the

228
00:20:52.302 --> 00:20:56.519
person involved. Can the student
communicate more comfortably? Can the

229
00:20:56.520 --> 00:21:01.286
family prepare, ask a question or add
knowledge that might otherwise be missed?

230
00:21:01.287 --> 00:21:06.176
Keep those choices with your conversation
notes. Consultation becomes more useful

231
00:21:06.177 --> 00:21:10.455
when we adapt it to the people involved,
and then act on what we learn.

232
00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:16.819
Partnership becomes particularly important
when a student's capacity runs

233
00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:21.543
out. The terms on this slide are meltdown,
shutdown, and what the slide

234
00:21:21.544 --> 00:21:26.943
calls School Can't. That is, the student
cannot attend school. We are considering

235
00:21:26.944 --> 00:21:32.005
these through capacity, accumulated
demands and the parts of experience that

236
00:21:32.006 --> 00:21:37.135
may be difficult for others to see. These
experiences need a response without

237
00:21:37.136 --> 00:21:41.455
blame. Describing them as wilful
misbehaviour, or as a failure of

238
00:21:41.456 --> 00:21:45.977
parenting, can close down the
understanding we need. Home and school

239
00:21:45.978 --> 00:21:50.769
may see different parts of the student's
day. A difference between those

240
00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:56.102
observations is a reason to listen and
learn more. Bring back the questions from

241
00:21:56.103 --> 00:22:01.299
Sessions Two and Three. What has the day
been asking of this student? What may

242
00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:06.496
have accumulated? What information does
the student have, and what might their

243
00:22:06.497 --> 00:22:11.221
family know about recovery? Keep the focus
on safety, what preceded the

244
00:22:11.222 --> 00:22:16.485
situation, what supports recovery, and
what can change in the environment, task

245
00:22:16.486 --> 00:22:21.345
or response. Use the workshop's safety
guidance and the support processes

246
00:22:21.346 --> 00:22:26.340
relevant to your setting. The partnership
task here is to share information

247
00:22:26.341 --> 00:22:31.335
respectfully, listen carefully and
identify changes that may reduce demands

248
00:22:31.336 --> 00:22:33.832
and improve the student's experience.

249
00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:40.589
Here are two Positive Partnerships tools
that can help make those conversations

250
00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:45.617
concrete. On the left, the Working
Together Communication Tool asks how home

251
00:22:45.618 --> 00:22:50.847
and school will communicate, how often,
about what, and what the next steps and

252
00:22:50.848 --> 00:22:55.875
dates will be. You can discuss options
such as text, email, an app, phone, a

253
00:22:55.876 --> 00:23:00.770
communication book or face-to-face
conversation. Choose what is useful and

254
00:23:00.771 --> 00:23:05.798
workable together. On the right, the
Partnership Planning Template asks what

255
00:23:05.799 --> 00:23:11.095
has worked, what has been challenging and
what could help. It gives examples and

256
00:23:11.096 --> 00:23:16.057
space for your own ideas. You might use it
to explore meeting arrangements,

257
00:23:16.058 --> 00:23:21.555
feeling heard, communication or including
the young person's voice. For example, if

258
00:23:21.556 --> 00:23:26.516
a family says phone calls are hard to
manage during work, you could use the

259
00:23:26.517 --> 00:23:31.679
tools to discuss another option and agree
when it will be reviewed. That is an

260
00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:36.707
illustrative use of the prompts; the
family may suggest something different.

261
00:23:36.708 --> 00:23:41.870
Use the tools with people. Leave room for
their priorities to change what gets

262
00:23:41.871 --> 00:23:47.099
written. A completed page is useful when
it records a shared understanding that

263
00:23:47.100 --> 00:23:49.245
can guide the next conversation.

264
00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:55.654
The NCCD Meetings Toolkit is another
practical resource. NCCD stands for the

265
00:23:55.655 --> 00:24:00.810
Nationally Consistent Collection of Data
on School Students with Disability. You

266
00:24:00.811 --> 00:24:06.096
can find the toolkit on the official NCCD
website. Its sections support requesting

267
00:24:06.097 --> 00:24:11.056
and managing meetings, being prepared,
managing emotions, keeping records and

268
00:24:11.057 --> 00:24:15.885
considering whether a meeting was
successful. Use it to make the purpose of

269
00:24:15.886 --> 00:24:20.780
a meeting, the consultation process,
possible adjustments, evidence and next

270
00:24:20.781 --> 00:24:26.001
steps clear. Explain responsibilities and
follow-up in a way that the student and

271
00:24:26.002 --> 00:24:30.961
family can understand. People need to know
why a meeting is happening, who is

272
00:24:30.962 --> 00:24:35.987
involved and how they can contribute.
Follow the processes used in your school

273
00:24:35.988 --> 00:24:40.816
or system, and use current official NCCD
information when you need specific

274
00:24:40.817 --> 00:24:45.776
guidance. The toolkit gives you a way to
prepare and review the process while

275
00:24:45.777 --> 00:24:49.691
keeping the discussion connected to the
student's experience.

276
00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:56.664
There are also practical support pathways
beyond today's workshop. Page thirty of

277
00:24:56.665 --> 00:25:01.719
your Participant Guide brings the
resources together. Positive Partnerships

278
00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:06.978
provides resources and learning options
for educators and families. This slide

279
00:25:06.979 --> 00:25:12.375
also shows Autism Connect, the national
autism information helpline, with phone,

280
00:25:12.376 --> 00:25:17.634
email and webchat options. Disability
Gateway can help people with disability,

281
00:25:17.635 --> 00:25:23.099
families and carers find information and
services. Carer Gateway provides support

282
00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:28.427
for carers. The contact details are on the
slide, so pause if you would like to

283
00:25:28.428 --> 00:25:33.550
record them. Choose a pathway that fits
the person's question, and check its

284
00:25:33.551 --> 00:25:38.810
website for the available support. We can
make families aware of these options

285
00:25:38.811 --> 00:25:41.679
while listening to what they want and
need.

286
00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:49.557
Before we finish this session, let's
prepare the bridge to one purposeful

287
00:25:49.558 --> 00:25:54.706
conversation. Return to the student you
have in mind and the classroom design

288
00:25:54.707 --> 00:25:59.651
change on your My students card. Open page
twenty-five, Bridge to the next

289
00:25:59.652 --> 00:26:04.800
family conversation. Work through these
questions privately. What needs to be

290
00:26:04.801 --> 00:26:09.881
understood? What question will open the
conversation? How will the student's

291
00:26:09.882 --> 00:26:15.030
voice be included? What can be decided
together? How and when will the school

292
00:26:15.031 --> 00:26:19.975
follow up? Bring in the process change you
chose during the power-dynamics

293
00:26:19.976 --> 00:26:24.989
activity. Make it part of how the
conversation will happen, as well as what

294
00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:30.476
you will ask. You are preparing your own
purpose and approach. Leave space for the

295
00:26:30.477 --> 00:26:35.422
student's and family's knowledge to change
your understanding and the next

296
00:26:35.423 --> 00:26:40.706
step. Keep names out of your workshop
notes. Allow about four minutes to write.

297
00:26:40.707 --> 00:26:45.516
Pause the video now, and resume when
you're ready to close this session.

298
00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:58.590
Take a final look at the question you plan
to open with. Does it invite the

299
00:26:58.591 --> 00:27:03.866
other person's knowledge? Have you left
something meaningful to understand and

300
00:27:03.867 --> 00:27:09.006
decide together? Keep these notes. In
Session Six, you'll use them to plan a

301
00:27:09.007 --> 00:27:14.076
real conversation on page twenty-eight,
alongside one classroom action. The

302
00:27:14.077 --> 00:27:19.558
person you named under who I will involve
in Session Four now has a place in that

303
00:27:19.559 --> 00:27:24.629
plan. We'll bring the day together through
belonging, capacity and joy, and

304
00:27:24.630 --> 00:27:29.974
choose the practical next steps you want
to carry back to school. Thank you for

305
00:27:29.975 --> 00:27:34.153
the care you've brought to thinking about
these relationships.
